  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nA few island band showers developed downwind of San Clemente\nIsland but remained over the coastal waters. These showers are now\nshifting south off the coast of Baja as flow turns more northerly.\nPressure gradients are trending offshore, currently around -1 mb\nLAX-DAG. Isolated gusts of 25-30 mph are being reported in the\nCajon Pass and northern Santa Ana Mountains. Pressure gradients\nand winds will strengthen through the morning with peak gusts\naround 30-40 mph in the wind-prone passes and canyons. Otherwise a\nsunny but cool day is in store with high temperatures around 5-10\ndegrees below normal away from the coast, and near normal at the\ncoast. Onshore flow returns on Sunday as another low pressure\nsystem begins to move in.\n\nEnsembles have trended faster with the arrival of the next short-\nwave trough, which is now expected to move in by Sunday evening.\nEnsembles have also trended wetter - at least in terms of the\nnumber of members showing precipitation. PoPs have increased to\n25-50% for the mountains westward Sunday evening into Sunday\nnight, with showers tapering off by Monday morning. Overall\nrainfall amounts remain light: NBM probabilities of at least 0.10\"\nare around 25-40%, and probabilities of at least 0.25\" are around\n10-25%. Snow levels will be near 5500-6000 ft, but given the lack\nof liquid equivalent, so totals will generally be less than 1\".\n\nWeak transitory ridging builds in behind this wave for the\nremainder of the day Monday and Tuesday for dry weather. A touch\nof weak offshore flow develops again Tuesday morning to bring some\nadditional warming west of the mountains and high temperatures\nnear to a couple degrees below normal across the region.\n\nWednesday's weather remains questionable as the evolution of a\nshort wave moving through the mean flow continues to differ\namongst the models. The GFS and about 50% of the GEFS members\nbegin to dig this wave much further west and is more amplified for\ncooler weather and a better chance of precipitation. However, \nthis remains the minority solution and only accounts for 18% of \nthe total ensemble space. The remaining 82% have a lower amplitude\nand more inside track, maintaining dry weather with less cooling.\nOverall trend has been towards a drier solution as less ensemble \nmembers are showing precipitation now. \n\nAs we head into Thanksgiving, upper level high pressure begins to\nbuild in from the west and surface high pressure settles into the\nGreat Basin, driving another round of offshore flow. Depending on\nhow the upper wave evolves, we may get some upper level support \nfor stronger winds, but again this is the outlier GFS-like \nsolution. The CW3E West-WRF has a 50% chance of weak Santa Ana \nwinds on Thanksgiving, and a 10% of weak to moderate winds. For\nThanksgiving, high temperatures are forecast to be around 2-5\ndegrees above normal, increasing to 5-10 degrees above normal on\nFriday as the upper ridge strengthens and offshore flow continues.\nThe ridge weakens and shifts east on Saturday as another waves\nmoves inland to our north, bringing cooling but otherwise dry\nweather. The pattern remains active with potential for more\nprecipitation the following week.",
